# Conventions

## Directory surface

A directory's public surface is its `mod.ts` (`mod.tsx` when the file
contains JSX). Import that file from outside the directory. Same-directory
imports are fine.

## Static JSON

A known static `.json` file is imported as a module (`import x from
"./foo.json" with { type: "json" }`). `JSON.parse` is for payloads that
arrive at runtime.

## Root import map

Root `deno.json` `imports` map a bare specifier to a published dependency.
`dashi` is `jsr:@cookingpot/dashi`. Do not path-override onto a framework
checkout. App dependencies are allowed here (Tailwind, later a markdown
parser).

Import `dashi` / `dashi/jsx-runtime` / `dashi/client` only.

## Root JSX config

Root `deno.json` sets JSX `precompile` and `jsxImportSource: dashi`. Do not
set `compilerOptions.lib`. JSX types are imported from `dashi/jsx-runtime`.

## Route modules

Page folders export handlers from `mod.ts` (`mod.tsx` when the file contains
JSX), not a `Group`. `home/mod.tsx` exports `Home`. Later pages are the same
(`Docs` from `docs/`). The root table in `main.ts` is the only place that
calls `route()` and names the path and methods. One-offs (`staticFile`) stay
on `main.ts`. Pathless `group()` is a wrap shell, not the `/` page. Shared
values live in a module that is not a route.

Application code never calls a handler. The router is what invokes one: a
direct call skips the target's middleware and error boundary, applies its
cache policy to the caller's response, and leaves it reading the caller's
`ctx`. Share markup as a component, including between two methods of one
route, and use `<RouteFragment src>` to include another route's rendered
output.

## Styling

Tailwind v4 utilities go on `className`. There is no `class`, `tw`, or `css`
prop, and no CSS modules or styled-components. `style` is a CSS string.

Source is `styles.css` at the app root (`@import "tailwindcss"` and
`@source`). `css.ts` runs `@tailwindcss/cli`, SHA-256s the bytes, writes
`generated/styles-<hash>.css`, and writes `styles.json` as
`{ "href": "/generated/styles-<hash>.css" }`. The hash alphabet matches dashi
client JS: `+` → `-`, `/` → `_`, `=` stripped. Layout imports the JSON and
emits `<link rel="stylesheet" href={styles.href} />`.

Hashed sheets are `GET /generated/:file` with `CacheStrategy.Immutable`.
Unhashed files (logo, favicon) are `GET /static/:file` with
`CacheStrategy.Immutable`. Both params are one segment. Do not serve
`styles.css`. `styles.json` and `generated/styles-*.css` are gitignored; run
`css` before type-check and serve.

## Discriminants

A closed set of cases is a `const enum`. Comparisons use the enum member, not
a string literal. Use a plain `enum` only when a const enum cannot (it must
exist at runtime as an object).

## Object types

An object shape is an `interface`. `type` is for unions, aliases, mapped
types, and everything that is not a single object shape.

## Type assertions

Do not assert (`as`) to silence the checker. An assertion is allowed when
the types cannot express the fact, and only at the use that needs it, with
a note saying why. Prefer restructuring so the assertion is unnecessary.

## Helpers

Do not extract a function that is a short, obvious check or a few
straightforward lines used once. Inline it until extraction earns its keep.
Extract when the same non-trivial shape is repeated, or when the body is long
or not obvious (`withoutContent`). A one-line env read used once stays at the
call site.

## Conditionals

Do not chain ternary operators. Use `if`.

## Client JS

Client TypeScript attaches only via `client.module` / `client.element` at
module scope. Browser-safe APIs import from `dashi/client`; client modules
do not import `dashi`. The factory throws after compile. App files are `*_client.ts`
and sit beside the module that registers them. The factory returns a
PascalCase component; `client.element` takes the custom element tag
(kebab-case). Compiled files are served at `/_dashi/client/` by a reserved
table route. Public URLs are flat `/_dashi/client/<name>-<hash>.js`.
Relative imports in compiled files are rewritten to the matching
bundler path. The import map is bundler path → flat hashed URL.
Documents get one `<script type="importmap">` for that graph;
a module script is added only when a host rendered. A lazy fragment
`import()`s its `Link` modulepreloads before swap.
`DASHI_MINIFY_CLIENT=1` minifies the bundle; `deno task dev` sets it to `0`.
`staticFile` is app-mounted disk files. `/_dashi/*` is reserved.
Client code names the DOM the way the HTML spec does. Prefer IDL properties
(`form.method`, `submitter.formAction`): their reflection rules, meaning
value normalisation, URL resolution, and missing-value defaults, are the
browser behaviour being mirrored. Use `getAttribute` or `hasAttribute`
when presence itself is the question. JSX prop spellings (`className`,
`htmlFor`) stop at the runtime's `MAPPED_HTML_ATTR_NAMES` and never appear
in client code.
`mod.ts` is the server surface of a directory; client runtime modules are
imported by file. One document-level submit listener owns interception.
There is no per-element submit listener and no nearest-host targeting. GET
navigates the page; a write goes through the registry. An applied patch-list
write resets the submitting form. Client features assume the client runtime.
